<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
  <h2>You&rsquo;ve seen them solve these problems at the kitchen table</h2>

  <p>Your teen knows the material. When there&rsquo;s no timer and no stakes, they work through it fine.</p>

  <p>Then the score comes back and it doesn&rsquo;t match. So you try the obvious things. More tutoring. More practice tests. More hours at the desk. And the number barely moves.</p>

  <p>Here&rsquo;s what&rsquo;s usually happening. The problem isn&rsquo;t how much your teen knows. Under pressure, their body registers the test as a threat: heart rate up, breathing shallow, blood flow shifting away from the part of the brain that handles complex reasoning. The knowledge is still there. Access to it isn&rsquo;t.</p>

  <p>More content review doesn&rsquo;t fix that. Nothing in traditional prep is aimed at it.</p>

  <p>And the cost isn&rsquo;t only the score. Test day after test day, a teen sits down knowing what they&rsquo;re capable of and walks out knowing they didn&rsquo;t show it. Eventually they stop reading that as a bad day and start reading it as a fact about themselves.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">The mechanism</p>
  <h2>The part of test performance that prep companies don&rsquo;t train</h2>

  <p>Test day performance runs on three systems: what your teen knows, how they feel under pressure, and what their body does during stress. Traditional prep trains the first one.</p>

  <p>We train all three, and we start with the physiological one, because it sits upstream of the other two. Between 85 and 90 percent of the nerve fibers connecting the heart and brain carry signals <em>upward</em>, from heart to brain. State shapes thinking, not the other way around.</p>

  <p>That&rsquo;s also why telling an anxious teen to relax doesn&rsquo;t work. You can&rsquo;t think your way out of a stress response once it&rsquo;s running. The body has to shift first.</p>

  <p>The method is heart-rate-variability coherence training, developed and researched by the HeartMath Institute over thirty years. It&rsquo;s a specific technique a teen can learn and repeat, and it takes two to three minutes. Nobody is asking them to adopt a mindset. On a monitor, a teen can watch their own heart rhythm go from jagged to smooth while they do it. That&rsquo;s usually the moment the skepticism ends.</p>

